---
/**
 * website/src/components/Downloads.astro — the console's downloads, one card per platform
 * (website plan, Task W1.6; the owner's approved board `website/design-ref/ConsoleV2.dc.html`,
 * 2026-10-20).
 *
 * This is the section the spec asks for as *"per-OS download links"*, and the console has
 * **shipped**: `console/` holds the application, its packaging and the workflow that signs,
 * notarises and staples its macOS artifacts, that workflow created the `console-v0.9.0`
 * release, and `site.yaml`'s `features.console_downloads` is true — so the section renders the
 * release's own downloads (W3.1, 2026-10-19). The placeholder it used to render is still here,
 * and it is what the unreleased branch below is: while `available` is false the section renders
 * one sentence and nothing a reader can follow, which is the state a copy of the repository is
 * built into and a test holds (`website/test/console.mjs`), rather than the state this checkout
 * ships (finding 23, finding 25, D95, Global Constraint 9).
 *
 * **The rule is structural, not editorial.** The two states are one `if`: the cards are
 * rendered from `downloads.json`'s `downloads` array and *only* when `available` is true, and
 * the unavailable branch has no element a reader can follow. That is what makes "the
 * placeholder carries no link and no button" a property of the component rather than a promise
 * about its copy — and the module that reads the data file refuses, in the build, a flag that
 * disagrees with `site.yaml`'s (W1.6 steps 1 and 2).
 *
 * **One card per platform, and a platform with nothing to download says so.** The board draws
 * three cards — the platform the release ships for, and the two it does not — and the two it
 * does not carry a "Not offered" pill and **no sentence of this file's own**: what a reader is
 * owed about them is already the data file's sentence at the top of the section
 * (`released.text`: "Windows and Linux builds do not exist yet."), and a card that repeated it,
 * or that wrote the board's own "A Windows build does not exist yet.", would be this page
 * saying a fact a second time in its own words (F37, Global Constraint 6). The platforms a card
 * is drawn for are held to that sentence below, so the page can never name a platform no
 * document of this repository speaks about.
 *
 * **The URLs are derived, not stored.** Each entry carries the name the release *serves* its
 * asset under — the release's own files spell `Fugaro Console-X.Y.Z-universal.dmg` and GitHub
 * serves `Fugaro.Console-X.Y.Z-universal.dmg` — and `src/lib/console-downloads.mjs` composes
 * the URL from it, from the release's tag and from `site.yaml`'s repository. So no file under
 * `website/` carries a download URL at all, and a version bump moves every button (the owner's
 * changes spec of 2026-10-18, item 6; Global Constraint 9).
 *
 * **The label is the platform and the asset's own extension** (`macOS .dmg`), which the loader
 * derives from the entry's `platform` and its file name: the site invents no distribution
 * (D4, D11). The board draws the same two labels as buttons, and they are the same two strings.
 *
 * **What a reader gets.** The release's own sentence about the console (macOS only, one
 * universal disk image and one archive, signed and Apple-notarised, the CLI first because the
 * CLI is the data plane) and one link per published asset with the SHA-256 the release recorded
 * for its bytes beside it. All of it comes from the data file: the checksum is the one fact
 * here that is not derived, because it is a fact about bytes and no build can compute it.
 *
 * **The section carried a verification command until 2026-10-21 and does not any more.** It
 * printed `shasum -a 256 Fugaro.Console-*` in a command block under the cards (P6's shape), and
 * the owner, reading the live page on 2026-10-21, struck it: *"nuke this part: `shasum -a 256
 * Fugaro.Console-*`"*. So the command block, its copy control and the `released.verify` line the
 * data file carried are all gone, and **the checksums stay** — each one is still printed beside
 * the link it belongs to, which is what a reader compares a download against. The data file
 * refuses the key coming back (`src/lib/console-downloads.mjs`), so a round that wants the
 * command again is a round that changes this file, the loader and this comment together.
 *
 * The facts come in as props rather than being read here, because the module owns the one
 * reader for them: `website/src/pages/console.astro` loads and validates
 * `website/content/downloads.json`, and this component renders what it was handed.
 */

interface DownloadFacts {
  /**
   * The data file's own id for the asset (`macos-dmg`): the key a per-platform block's
   * `assets` list names, which `console-downloads.mjs` resolves against this list. The
   * section filters on it when it draws a block's own rows under that block rather than
   * above it, so telling one row from another never needs the file names to be compared.
   */
  id: string
  /** What a reader sees on the link: the platform and the asset's extension (`macOS .dmg`). */
  label: string
  /** Where a reader downloads it from. Only ever rendered while `available` is true. */
  url: string
  /** The SHA-256 of the published bytes, printed beside the link to check a download with. */
  sha256: string
  /** The platform the asset is built for (`macOS`), which is the card it is drawn on. */
  platform: string
}

/**
 * A platform whose own artifact exists but whose facts the released sentence above does not
 * describe: Linux, whose build is unsigned and whose files are an AppImage and a `.deb` rather
 * than disk images (the owner's ruling of 2026-10-20; `docs/design/desktop-console.md` D10).
 */
interface PlatformFacts {
  /** The data file's own sentence for this platform — rendered, never restated here. */
  text: string
  /**
   * The data file's own entries for the assets this block links, in the file's order. Every id
   * the block names in `downloads.json` has been resolved to an entry of the file's `downloads`
   * list by `src/lib/console-downloads.mjs`, so a link here is always a link to an asset the
   * data file names — and the file refuses a block naming an id it does not carry.
   */
  downloads: DownloadFacts[]
}

interface Props {
  /** `downloads.json`'s `available`: the console's own artifact exists (W3.1 flipped it). */
  available: boolean
  /** The data file's `not_released` sentence, rendered while `available` is false. */
  note: string
  /**
   * The data file's `released` block: what the console is once it has shipped (macOS only,
   * signed and notarised, the CLI first). Rendered while `available` is true, and rendered
   * from the data file rather than written here.
   */
  released: { text: string; source: string }
  /**
   * The data file's `downloads` entries: the release's published names, which the data file
   * carries from the day they are known and not only once the console ships. They are rendered
   * while `available` is true and never otherwise, which is what makes the unreleased state
   * structural rather than editorial.
   */
  downloads: DownloadFacts[]
  /**
   * The data file's optional per-platform block, or `null` when it carries none — which is the
   * state this checkout ships. It is rendered **only** when it is there, and while it is not
   * there this component draws no block for it: no sentence, no link, and no word saying which
   * platform is missing beyond the card's own name and its pill. So the page's sentence about
   * the platform the release carries stays the only one it makes, without the markup having to
   * say "and not Linux" (`src/lib/console-downloads.mjs`, D10).
   */
  linux: PlatformFacts | null
}

const { available, note, released, downloads, linux } = Astro.props

// A label rather than a fact: the section's own heading, which is the navigation's word
// for this page and not a claim about the product (the layout's nav carries the same one).
const HEADING = 'Downloads'

/**
 * What a card with nothing to download says. It is the page's own word — the board draws it and
 * it is the shortest true thing to say about a platform the release does not carry — and it is
 * listed with the page's other new labels in the pull request for the owner to veto. It is a
 * pill's label and not a sentence: the reason is the data file's own sentence above the cards.
 */
const NOT_OFFERED = 'Not offered'

/**
 * The platforms this section draws a card for and the release carries no asset for. It is this
 * file's own list — the board draws both cards — and it is only safe because of the guard
 * below: each name is held to the data file's own sentence about the state of the release, so a
 * card here can never be about a platform no document of this repository mentions.
 */
const WITHOUT_ARTIFACTS = ['Windows', 'Linux']

// The assets a platform block links are rendered *under that block* and not in the cards above
// it: one asset of a release is one card, and a Linux artifact listed twice — once by the block
// that explains it and once by the sentence about the disk images — would be two links to one
// file with one of them under a sentence that does not describe it. With no block the cards
// above are every asset the data file names, which is what the section renders today.
const inBlock = new Set((linux?.downloads ?? []).map((download) => download.id))
const listedAbove = downloads.filter((download) => !inBlock.has(download.id))

/** The platforms the release's own entries are built for, once each and in the file's order. */
const offered = [...new Set(listedAbove.map((download) => download.platform))]

/** The platforms the release carries nothing for, minus the one a block of its own explains. */
const missing = WITHOUT_ARTIFACTS.filter(
  (platform) => !offered.includes(platform) && !(platform === 'Linux' && linux),
)

// Hold every card to the data file's own sentence about the release. The cards below are the
// board's own three, and a card is a claim about a platform: the sentence the section renders
// above them is where a reader is told why two of them have nothing to download. So a platform
// this file draws a card for but that sentence does not name is the page saying something no
// document of this repository says (Global Constraint 6) — and the day the release carries a
// platform the sentence has stopped mentioning, this is what stops the build rather than
// shipping a card with no reason above it.
if (available) {
  for (const platform of missing) {
    if (!released.text.includes(platform)) {
      throw new Error(
        `website/src/components/Downloads.astro: the section draws a "${NOT_OFFERED}" card for ${platform} and ${JSON.stringify(released.text)} — the sentence it renders above the cards — does not name ${platform}: a card about a platform no document of this repository speaks about is a claim with nothing behind it (Global Constraint 6). Either the release's sentence was reworded or the card moved off it.`,
      )
    }
  }
}

/**
 * One card: a platform the release carries assets for (its rows and their checksums), the
 * platform a block of the data file explains (the block's sentence and its rows), or a platform
 * the release carries nothing for (the card's name and the pill, and nothing else).
 */
type Card =
  | { platform: string; kind: 'offered'; downloads: DownloadFacts[] }
  | { platform: string; kind: 'block'; text: string; downloads: DownloadFacts[] }
  | { platform: string; kind: 'not-offered' }

// The board's own order while the release carries one platform: the platform it ships for,
// then the two it does not. A block of the data file is a platform that HAS assets, so it is
// drawn beside the other offered one rather than at the end of the row.
const cards: Card[] = [
  ...offered.map((platform) => ({
    platform,
    kind: 'offered' as const,
    downloads: listedAbove.filter((download) => download.platform === platform),
  })),
  ...(linux ? [{ platform: 'Linux', kind: 'block' as const, text: linux.text, downloads: linux.downloads }] : []),
  ...missing.map((platform) => ({ platform, kind: 'not-offered' as const })),
]
---

<section id="downloads" aria-labelledby="downloads-heading">
  <h2 id="downloads-heading" class="display-lg">{HEADING}</h2>

  {
    available ? (
      <>
        {/* What the console is now that it has shipped: the platform, the signing and
            notarisation, and the CLI-first rule — the data file's own sentence, rendered
            rather than written here. It is also the reason the cards below that carry no
            download carry none, which is why no card repeats it. */}
        <p class="downloads-released">{released.text}</p>

        <ul class="download-cards">
          {cards.map((card) => (
            <li class="download-card card">
              <div class="download-card-head">
                <h3 class="download-card-platform">{card.platform}</h3>
                {/* The one card state that is not a link or a download: the platform the
                    release does not carry, said in one pill and no sentence of this file's
                    own (the data file's sentence above is where the reason is). */}
                {card.kind === 'not-offered' && (
                  <span class="pill download-card-status">{NOT_OFFERED}</span>
                )}
              </div>

              {card.kind === 'offered' && (
                <ul class="downloads">
                  {card.downloads.map((download) => (
                    <li class="download-row">
                      <a class="download-link" href={download.url} rel="noopener">
                        {download.label}
                      </a>{' '}
                      {/* The published bytes' own checksum, beside the link it belongs to: a
                          reader who has the file can see whether what arrived is what the
                          release published, without trusting the transfer. The space above is
                          a fact and not a style: the two elements are inline, so a
                          whitespace-only text node between them is what Astro's compression
                          drops — and the row would read "macOS .dmga434f3…"
                          (website/test/spacing.mjs holds it). */}
                      <code class="download-sha256">{download.sha256}</code>
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              )}

              {/* A platform whose artifact exists and whose facts the sentence above does not
                  carry — Linux, unsigned, an AppImage and a `.deb` (D10). One expression and one
                  condition: while the data file carries no block for it this renders nothing at
                  all, and the rows it does render are the same rows as the cards above, from
                  the same entries, so a link here can only ever be an asset the data file
                  names. The `<div>` is the element `website/test/console.mjs` reads the block
                  as, and its list is the last thing in it. */}
              {card.kind === 'block' && (
                <div class="downloads-linux">
                  <p>{card.text}</p>
                  <ul class="downloads">
                    {card.downloads.map((download) => (
                      <li class="download-row">
                        <a class="download-link" href={download.url} rel="noopener">
                          {download.label}
                        </a>{' '}
                        <code class="download-sha256">{download.sha256}</code>
                      </li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </div>
              )}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </>
    ) : (
      <div class="downloads-unreleased">
        {/* One paragraph, and nothing else: while the console is unreleased this branch
            renders no `<a>` and no `<button>` — not because a rule says not to, but
            because there is no element here that could be one. That is the red step's
            fix, and website/test/console.mjs asserts it over the built page. */}
        <p>{note}</p>
      </div>
    )
  }
</section>

<style>
  /* Tokens only (W1.2): every colour below is the palette in force, so the contrast check
     in `website/test/a11y.mjs` is about the colours this section actually paints. */
  .downloads-released {
    max-width: 60ch;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
  }

  /* The cards: one per platform, wrapping to one column on a phone. The grid is the site's
     own shape (`repeat(auto-fit, minmax(…, 1fr))`, as the home page's two grids are), so a
     second platform's card needs no rule of its own. It is what a viewport with no room for
     the cards side by side gets, and the wide block below is what a desktop gets. */
  .download-cards {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(17rem, 1fr));
    /* Equal heights: the cards of one row are one row of a grid, so they are as tall as the
       tallest of them rather than as tall as their own text. Stated rather than left to the
       grid's own default, because it is the thing the owner read the cards for on 2026-10-21
       ("the cards should also keep equal heights") and a declaration is what a gate can read
       (`website/test/console.mjs`). */
    align-items: stretch;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* **One row on a desktop** (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-21: the platform cards "should
     fit in ONE row on desktop (wrap only on narrow screens)"). Every card the data file draws
     goes on one line at the same width, however many there are: no minimum column width to
     run out of, and no count written here for the data file to outgrow. Below this width the
     base rule above wraps them, which is what a phone and a narrow window get. */
  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .download-cards {
      grid-template-columns: none;
      grid-auto-flow: column;
      grid-auto-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }

  .download-card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .download-card-head {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .download-card-platform {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
    font-weight: var(--weight-black);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
  }

  /* The pill the shared look already draws, in the warning tone: a platform the release does
     not carry is the one card state a reader should be able to see from across the page. The
     pair (`--warn` on `--panel`) is in `website/test/a11y.mjs`'s contrast set, so it is
     computed rather than assumed. */
  .download-card-status {
    border-color: var(--warn);
    color: var(--warn);
  }

  /* The rows of a card: one asset each, its label and its checksum. The rows are the same
     markup in an offered card and in a block, because they are the same fact. */
  .downloads {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .download-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  /* The label is the link, and it is the accent's legible form — the same tone every other
     link on the site is. Its box is at least 44 px tall so a thumb can hit it, which is the
     one measurement the owner's brief of 2026-10-19 asks of every control. */
  .download-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    color: var(--accent-on-dark);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  /* The checksum: 64 characters with nowhere to break, so its rule is about wrapping rather
     than about hiding it — the owner's feedback of 2026-10-21 was that the cards should "not
     show the long checksum text breaking awkwardly (keep the checksum, wrap it cleanly with
     word-break and a monospace smaller size)". So it is `word-break: break-all` (the same
     declaration as before, kept and now held by a gate), the monospace stack rather than the
     page's sans, one step below the running text's size, and it takes its own line under the
     link it belongs to (`flex-basis: 100%` in the row above): a hash that starts where the
     link ends breaks into columns of whatever room is left, which is the "awkwardly" the
     owner read. The family is stated rather than inherited from `code`'s own rule in
     `base.css`, because this is the one run of text on the page whose job is character-by-
     character comparison. */
  .download-sha256 {
    flex: 1 1 100%;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xs);
    word-break: break-all;
  }

  /* The block a platform with its own sentence is drawn in: a rule down its left, so the
     sentence and the rows under it read as one thing inside the card. */
  .downloads-linux {
    padding-left: var(--space-sm);
    border-left: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
  }

  .downloads-linux > p {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2xs);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .downloads-unreleased > p {
    max-width: 60ch;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
